 ## Overview
-NeRF Editing and Inpainting Procedures modify a pre-trained Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) to remove objects, change appearance, or fill occluded regions while maintaining multi-view consistency. Unlike standard training which optimizes for photometric fidelity on input views, these methods introduce external priors (2D inpainting, diffusion models, semantic features) or geometric constraints to guide the optimization of specific regions. They are distinguished from [NeRF Training Loop](970-nerf-training-loop.md) by their focus on localized 3D manipulation rather than global reconstruction, and from [Differentiable and inverse volume rendering](1200-differentiable-inverse-volume-rendering.md) by their reliance on external generative or semantic guidance rather than pure gradient descent on pixel errors alone.
+NeRF Editing and Inpainting Procedures modify a pre-trained Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) to alter its appearance, geometry, or content while maintaining multi-view consistency. This family solves the problem of localized 3D content manipulation—such as object removal, style transfer, or filling occluded regions—by optimizing NeRF parameters or querying auxiliary fields based on external 2D priors (e.g., diffusion models, semantic segmentations) or geometric constraints. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [Training Ray Marching and Integration](2199-training-ray-marching-and-integration.md) is the explicit integration of external guidance or deterministic transformation pipelines into the rendering or optimization loop, rather than merely reconstructing the scene from raw input images.
 
